    {
      "id": "shgIyHjttD_pbrT3kQ8bY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 809.1946773789159,
      "y": 359.453939912215,
      "width": 3524.398193359375,
      "height": 405,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bJV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1039086035,
      "version": 1290,
      "versionNonce": 1206710005,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1753082063603,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Although this seems to be the most relevant brute force method for me it may differ for others. The idea is quite simple tbh and can be cracked by Looking at the examples itself \nso the basic problem i came across with while deducing a solution was observing that a element with overall frequency in the string < k can never be part\nof any of the possible substrings. In order to solve this problem we have to solve this subproblem \n\nHow can we solve this problem of freq(char) < k in string  ?????   ===>>>  Instantly I came up with approach where i would divide a given string across such character\n\n**(Knowledge of how merge sort internally works helped me implement this solution)\n\n ",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Although this seems to be the most relevant brute force method for me it may differ for others. The idea is quite simple tbh and can be cracked by Looking at the examples itself \nso the basic problem i came across with while deducing a solution was observing that a element with overall frequency in the string < k can never be part\nof any of the possible substrings. In order to solve this problem we have to solve this subproblem \n\nHow can we solve this problem of freq(char) < k in string  ?????   ===>>>  Instantly I came up with approach where i would divide a given string across such character\n\n**(Knowledge of how merge sort internally works helped me implement this solution)\n\n ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "WnQCWcf9t2iKMjZdnzT0O",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1023.6354095975644,
      "y": 905.4965396652515,
      "width": 1950.216064453125,
